WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.046
Six Cassandra nodes, none in charge.

2
00:00:05.046 --> 00:00:08.175
Each one must still know which others are alive.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:15.425
Every node could ping every other node each second: 30 messages for six nodes, a million for a thousand.

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.331
Cassandra gossips instead.

5
00:00:17.331 --> 00:00:25.256
Each node keeps a row per node: a generation, when that node started, and a version, a heartbeat it bumps every second.

6
00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:28.248
Each second, a node picks a random peer.

7
00:00:28.248 --> 00:00:33.672
Node 1 picks node 4 and sends a Syn: its generation and version for each node.

8
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:41.349
Node 4 is ahead on nodes 4 and 6, so its Ack carries those rows, and asks for 1 and 3, where it's behind.

9
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:43.586
Node 1 sends those in an Ack2.

10
00:00:43.586 --> 00:00:47.489
Three messages, and both now hold the newest of every row.

11
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.282
Follow one change: node 1 bumps its version to 53.

12
00:00:51.282 --> 00:00:55.223
Each round, every node that has it tells a random peer.

13
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:58.433
It spreads to two, then four, then all six.

14
00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:03.873
The count doubles, so it takes about log two of N rounds, ten for 1,000 nodes.

15
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.305
Now node 5 stops.

16
00:01:05.305 --> 00:01:09.276
Its version freezes, while the other rows keep counting.

17
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.861
A fixed timeout fires too early on a slow network.

18
00:01:12.861 --> 00:01:20.457
So each node runs a phi accrual detector, which learns how often heartbeats arrive and scores the silence as phi.

19
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:25.221
A phi of 1 means a live node goes this quiet one time in ten.

20
00:01:25.221 --> 00:01:29.327
Phi 8, the default threshold, means one in 100 million.

21
00:01:29.327 --> 00:01:31.865
Past it, node 1 marks node 5 down.

22
00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:35.459
Node 5 restarts with a later generation, 9:20.

23
00:01:35.459 --> 00:01:41.360
Its version starts low, but the later generation wins, so the others mark it up.

24
00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:46.277
A new node finds the cluster through seeds, a few addresses in its config.

25
00:01:46.277 --> 00:01:49.803
A seed is only the first node it calls, never a master.

26
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.087
One random peer a second, three short messages, and news reaches every node in a few rounds.
